<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ratchis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. after 757</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Ratchis of Friuli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ratchis was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> from 739 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a> from 744 to 749. His conciliatory policy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> angered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> nobles, and he abdicated to become a monk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, leaving the throne to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a>. He briefly tried to regain power in 756–757. The Altar of Ratchis in Cividale is a masterpiece of Lombard sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ratchis.jpg" alt="Ratchis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Welleschik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cividale_Ratchis1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
